<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Maroon War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1795">1795</a>–<a href="/years/1796">1796</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Maroon War of 1795 · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Maroon War of 1795 to 1796 was a conflict in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroons">Maroons</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trelawny-town">Trelawny Town</a> and the British colonial government under the Earl of Balcarres. After months of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> fighting in the Cockpit Country, the Maroons surrendered on terms, but the colonial authorities deported about 600 of them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a>; most later moved on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a> in 1800.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-maroon-war.jpg" alt="Second Maroon War" width="320" height="253" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%27Trelawney_Town,_the_Chief_Residence_of_the_Maroons%27_RMG_E9983.tiff" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroons">Maroons</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trelawny-town">Trelawny Town</a>, also called Cudjoe's Town, felt mistreated under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cudjoe">Cudjoe</a>'s Treaty of 1739, which had ended the First Maroon War. The spark came when a court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montego-bay">Montego Bay</a> convicted two Maroons, one of them Peter Campbell, of stealing two pigs and had them flogged by a black slave while other prisoners jeered; for half a century the Maroons had hunted runaway slaves. When six Maroon leaders, led by Montague James, came to present their grievances, they were taken prisoner on the orders of the new governor, Alexander Lindsay, 6th Earl of Balcarres, who wrongly believed the French had spread revolutionary ideas among them. Ignoring local planters, he sent forces against the town, and fighting began in mid-August 1795.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Windward Maroon towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moore-town">Moore Town</a>, Charles Town and Scott's Hall stayed neutral, while Accompong Town fought for the colonial authorities. The British fielded 5,000 troops and militia, outnumbering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroons">Maroons</a> ten to one, but the mountains and forests favoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war under officers such as Leonard Parkinson, James Palmer and John Jarrett. In the first two weeks the Maroons killed 65 British soldiers, and Colonel William Fitch was killed in an ambush; recent research suggests the militias killed only about 21 Trelawny Maroons. Hundreds of runaway slaves fought alongside them and may have outnumbered their warriors.</p><p>General George Walpole's scorched-earth campaign cut the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroons">Maroons</a> off from food, water and ammunition as the dry season began, and Balcarres brought in about a hundred bloodhounds with their handlers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>. In December 1795 the Maroons agreed terms with Walpole, who gave his word they would not be transported off the island.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The governor allowed only three days for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroons">Maroons</a> to beg forgiveness by 1 January 1796, and most, suspicious, did not come in until mid-March. Balcarres treated this as a breach of the treaty and deported most of them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a>. Walpole resigned in protest and denounced the governor in vain in the House of Commons. Seventeen died on the voyage and 19 in the first bitter winter, and in 1797 the surgeon John Oxley counted 550 in Nova Scotia. Led by Montague James, they later went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> was abolished in 1838, scores of Trelawny Town Maroons, Peter Campbell among them, came back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> as labourers and settled at Flagstaff.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Second Maroon War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In <b>the Second Maroon War</b> of 1795 to 1796 the Trelawny Town Maroons were deported to Nova Scotia and later Sierra Leone.</blockquote><a href="/state/jamaican-maroons">Jamaican Maroons</a></li></ul></section>
